On Fri, 29 Dec 2006 05:46:25 +0100, Patric Mueller <bhaak@bigfoot.com>
wrote:

> > Communal cooperation is
> > frequently a smarter, more efficient alternative to competition.
> 
> Maybe (although I doubt it in regard to game development).
> 
> But what should we do if we would want to change the development model
> of the official NetHack and the DevTeam just refuses to play along?
> 
> What do you suggest us to do in this case?


If enough players - particularly more influential players who are
well-versed in the game, write patches for the community, etc. -
activate for change, the current devteam is put under considerable
pressure to act themselves.  Failure to serve the wishes of the player
community would hasten their obsolescence, regardless of naming
issues.

This is also a key point.  The community has the collective resources,
talent, and time to develop our game, if motivated to.  The current
devteam serves at the community's pleasure.  I think we forget this
sometimes.

My inclination would be to continue to name the open project
"NetHack", but give it a version number several steps higher, or start
a different versioning system, to indicate the change in development;
and also to clearly explain these changes in the game's 'V'ersion
information.

Jim


